    "transcript": "Here is the paper's real data figure. Across runs each galaxy's stellar mass, and up runs its gas-oxygen abundance. These curves show the nearby benchmark relations. Now the five unlinsed galaxies appear, the red circles. Everyone sits below the benchmark, near one-fifth of the nearby level. In logarithmic units, the average shortfall is 0.69 decks, with a small spread. What if the benchmark itself misleads, swap it for one measured at exactly these masses? The shortfall barely moves, about 0.65 decks. One more check is fully independent. Roughly 1,500-fainter galaxies are stacked into one combined measurement. Back on the figure, the blue square is that stack, about 0.5 to 0.6 decks low. What could still bend this result? The absolute temperature-based abundance scale is debated at the level of 0.1 to 0.2 decks. That is smaller than the shortfall, but it limits how precisely we know its size. And only five galaxies carry the redshift 9-10 result. Machine-generated by an autonomous research pipeline, descriptive, not validated, automated review is not journal or human peer review. This is not a formal statistical detection.",
